\begin{abstract}
The Trichological Cycle Engine (TCE) presents a mathematically closed,
autonomous dynamical framework for modeling the human hair-follicle
lifecycle across Anagen, Catagen, and Telogen phases. The architecture
integrates a phase-coupled differential vitality equation, discrete
state-transition logic, bounded stochastic forcing, and a sovereign
supervisory kernel capable of autonomous fault interception and
recovery. A Lyapunov stability analysis demonstrates that the nominal
equilibrium $F^*$ resides within a compact attractor basin, ensuring
practical stability under perturbations satisfying $\|\Delta S(t)\|
\le \delta_{\max}$. Stress-mode overrides for mitotic acceleration,
apoptotic regression, temporal compression, and identity-token
saturation were evaluated through Monte-Carlo simulations, confirming
trajectory confinement within the stability boundary layer and
validating the efficacy of the sovereign purge operator
$\mathcal{P}_{\text{sov}}$. The TCE constitutes a new class of
biological computational architecture---an autonomous cycle engine---
providing a generalizable template for organ-level dynamical modeling,
long-duration simulation, and sovereign biological computation.
\end{abstract}